---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Community from '../../components/Community.astro';
import { links } from '../../config/site';

/**
 * Privacy policy for Noctis for Android, the URL given to Google Play
 * (https://noctisapp.cc/privacy/android). Android only: the desktop app makes
 * network requests this app does not (update check, lyrics and tag lookups), so the two
 * must not share a page. The wording mirrors store/android/privacy-policy.md on the
 * Android branch; keep them identical. Every claim was checked against the Android
 * build (SAF-only folder access, logcat-only diagnostics, the Last.fm album.getinfo
 * lookup in Noctis.Core LastFmAlbumDescriptions, the Deezer artist-photo lookup in
 * Noctis.Core DeezerArtistPhotos, the LRCLIB lyrics lookup in Noctis.Core LrcLibService, the
 * artist-logo lookup in Noctis.Core ArtistLogoService, the opt-in Last.fm / ListenBrainz
 * scrobbling in Noctis.Core ScrobbleClients, the optional account link).
 */
const updated = '4 November 2026';
---

<Base
  title="Noctis for Android · Privacy policy"
  description="Noctis for Android collects no data. No ads, no analytics, no crash reporting. Your library, lyrics and settings stay on your phone; album descriptions come from Last.fm, artist photos from Deezer and missing lyrics from LRCLIB."
  path="/privacy/android"
>
  <section class="pp section" aria-labelledby="pp-title">
    <div class="container pp__inner">
      <p class="eyebrow">Privacy policy · Noctis for Android</p>
      <h1 class="h2 pp__title" id="pp-title">Your music stays on your phone.</h1>
      <p class="lede pp__lede">
        Noctis for Android does not collect, sell or share any personal data or usage data.
        The developer receives nothing from the app: there is no advertising, no analytics,
        no crash reporting and no developer server. The app’s network connections are all
        described under Network: it asks Last.fm for album descriptions, sending only the
        album’s artist and title; it asks Deezer for artist photos, sending only the
        artist’s name; it asks LRCLIB for lyrics to songs that have none of their own,
        sending only the song’s title, artist and length; it may ask MusicBrainz, fanart.tv
        and TheAudioDB for artists’ logos, sending only the artist’s name and sometimes a
        few song titles; only if you connect Last.fm or ListenBrainz, it sends them the
        songs you play; and, only if you sign in, it connects to your own computer running
        the Noctis desktop app.
      </p>
      <p class="pp__meta">Last updated {updated}.</p>

      <h2 class="h3">What stays on your device</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>The folders you choose, and your phone’s music if you allow it.</strong>
          Noctis reads the folders you pick with Android’s folder picker. Only if you turn on
          Find All Music Automatically, it asks for Android’s music and audio permission (on
          Android 12 and older, permission to read storage) and reads the songs Android lists
          on your device, to add them to your library on the device; nothing from them leaves
          the device. It has no other storage or media permission. Turning the option off
          stops it; you can also take the permission back in Android’s settings. Uninstalling
          the app, or clearing its storage in Android’s settings, removes that access.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Your library index.</strong> Titles, artists, albums, artwork and lyrics
          read from the tags and lyric files in those folders are stored in the app’s
          private storage so the library opens quickly.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Your activity in the app.</strong> Favourites, playlists, play history,
          the play queue, a record of library changes and your settings are stored in the
          app’s private storage.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Album descriptions.</strong> Descriptions fetched from Last.fm (see
          Network) are kept in the app’s private storage so each album is looked up only
          once.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Artist photos.</strong> Photos fetched from Deezer (see Network) are kept
          in the app’s private storage so each artist is looked up only once, with a note of
          the artists Deezer had no photo for, so those are not asked about again for a few
          days.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Lyrics found online.</strong> Lyrics fetched from LRCLIB (see Network) are
          kept in the app’s private storage so each song is looked up only once.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Artist logos.</strong> Logos fetched for artist pages (see Network) are
          kept in the app’s private storage so each artist is looked up only once, with a
          note of the artists that had no logo, so those are not asked about again for a
          while.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Your scrobbling connections (only if you connect them).</strong> Your
          Last.fm user name and the session key Last.fm issued, or your ListenBrainz user
          name and the token you pasted, are stored in the app’s private storage, with the
          plays still waiting to be sent. Your Last.fm password is never entered in Noctis
          or stored.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Diagnostics.</strong> Noctis writes diagnostic messages to Android’s
          system log on your device, with account keys and passwords masked. They are never
          sent anywhere. If you use Settings → Export logs, the log is saved only to the
          file you choose; Noctis never sends it anywhere.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Your Noctis account link (only if you sign in).</strong> The address of
          your computer, your account name, the device key your computer issued, its
          certificate fingerprint and a random id for this install are stored in app-private
          storage that Android never backs up. Your password is never stored. Songs you
          download are kept in that same never-backed-up storage.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="h3">Network</h2>
      <p>
        Noctis contains no third-party SDKs, and the developer runs no server. It connects
        only to the places below.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Last.fm, for album descriptions.</strong> When you open an album page,
        Noctis asks Last.fm’s public web service (ws.audioscrobbler.com, run by Last.fm
        Limited) for a description of that album, over an encrypted (HTTPS) connection. The
        request contains only the album’s artist and title (if Last.fm has nothing for the
        full artist credit, Noctis asks once more with the first artist named) and Noctis’s
        own application key. It contains no account, no device id and nothing else about
        you, although, as with any website, Last.fm can see your device’s IP address. The
        answers are stored on your phone so each album is looked up only once. Last.fm’s
        privacy policy covers its service:
        <a href="https://www.last.fm/legal/privacy">https://www.last.fm/legal/privacy</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Deezer, for artist photos.</strong> When you open an artist page, or artists
        come into view in the Artists list, in search results or among your pinned items,
        Noctis asks Deezer’s public web service (api.deezer.com, run by Deezer SA) for a
        photo of each artist it does not have yet, over an encrypted (HTTPS) connection, and
        downloads the photo from Deezer’s image server (cdn-images.dzcdn.net). The request
        contains only the artist’s name (if Deezer has nothing for the full artist credit,
        Noctis asks once more with the first artist named): no account, no device id and
        nothing else about you, although, as with any website, Deezer can see your device’s
        IP address. When several Deezer artists share that name, Noctis also asks Deezer for
        those artists’ top songs and compares them with your library on your phone to pick
        the right one; your library is never sent. The photos are stored on your phone so
        each artist is looked up only once. Deezer’s privacy policy covers its service:
        <a href="https://www.deezer.com/legal/personal-datas">https://www.deezer.com/legal/personal-datas</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>LRCLIB, for lyrics.</strong> When you open the lyrics of a song that has no
        lyrics file and no lyrics in its tags, Noctis asks LRCLIB’s public web service
        (lrclib.net) for that song’s lyrics, over an encrypted (HTTPS) connection. The
        request contains only the song’s title, artist and length: no account, no device id
        and nothing else about you, although, as with any website, LRCLIB can see your
        device’s IP address. The lyrics found are stored on your phone so each song is
        looked up only once. Turn off Settings → Lyrics → Find Lyrics Online to stop these
        lookups. LRCLIB is a free, community-run service:
        <a href="https://lrclib.net">https://lrclib.net</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>MusicBrainz, fanart.tv and TheAudioDB, for artist logos.</strong> When you
        open an artist page, Noctis may look for that artist’s logo, over encrypted (HTTPS)
        connections. It asks MusicBrainz (musicbrainz.org, run by the MetaBrainz Foundation)
        which artist the name belongs to, sending only the artist’s name; when several
        artists share that name, it also sends up to four titles of that artist’s songs in
        your library so MusicBrainz can tell them apart. It then asks fanart.tv
        (webservice.fanart.tv) and TheAudioDB (www.theaudiodb.com) for that artist’s logo,
        sending only MusicBrainz’s id for the artist, or, if MusicBrainz cannot be reached,
        the artist’s name to TheAudioDB. No account, no device id and nothing else about you
        is sent, although, as with any website, these services can see your device’s IP
        address. MusicBrainz’s privacy policy covers its service:
        <a href="https://metabrainz.org/privacy">https://metabrainz.org/privacy</a>;
        fanart.tv and TheAudioDB describe theirs on their own sites:
        <a href="https://fanart.tv">https://fanart.tv</a> and
        <a href="https://www.theaudiodb.com">https://www.theaudiodb.com</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Last.fm and ListenBrainz, only if you connect them.</strong> Under Settings
        → Scrobbling you can connect Last.fm (you approve Noctis on Last.fm’s own website,
        which gives Noctis a session key) or ListenBrainz (you paste your user token). From
        then on, for each song you listen to past half its length or four minutes, Noctis
        sends its title, artist, album, length and the time you played it to the service you
        connected (ws.audioscrobbler.com for Last.fm, api.listenbrainz.org for
        ListenBrainz), over an encrypted (HTTPS) connection, and, unless you turn it off,
        the song you are playing now. These plays are stored in your account on that
        service, as you asked. Disconnect under Settings → Scrobbling to stop. Their privacy
        policies cover their services:
        <a href="https://www.last.fm/legal/privacy">https://www.last.fm/legal/privacy</a>
        and <a href="https://metabrainz.org/privacy">https://metabrainz.org/privacy</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Your own computer, only if you choose to.</strong> When you sign in under
        Settings → Account, Noctis connects to your own computer, running the Noctis desktop
        app.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Signing in.</strong> You enter your computer’s address. Noctis first shows
          the fingerprint of your computer’s security certificate so you can check it
          matches the one your computer shows, and only then sends your account name and
          password — once, over an encrypted (TLS) connection — to receive a key for this
          device. The key, not your password, is used from then on, and every later
          connection must present that same certificate.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Signing in with a QR code.</strong> Instead of typing, you can tap Scan QR
          Code and scan the code your computer shows. The code holds your computer’s address,
          its certificate fingerprint and a one-time sign-in code that expires after two
          minutes and works only once; it never contains your password. Noctis checks the
          certificate against the fingerprint before sending the one-time code. The scanner
          is Google’s code scanner, part of Google Play services: it runs on your phone and
          gives Noctis only the text of the code, so Noctis never gets camera access. Google
          may collect usage and diagnostic information about the scanner under the
          <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy">Google Privacy Policy</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>What goes to your computer.</strong> The device key, a random id and your
          phone’s model name (so your computer can list and remove this device), requests
          for your songs, covers and library, and the changes you make on the phone:
          favourites, ratings, playlists and plays.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>What comes back.</strong> Your library (song and album details), covers,
          playlists, favourites, ratings and play counts from your computer, and the music
          itself, streamed or downloaded.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        All of the account traffic travels only between your phone and your computer,
        encrypted. None of it goes to the developer or to any third party (apart from the
        QR scanner’s own usage information to Google described above, which contains none of
        your account traffic). Nothing is sent
        without an address you entered and a certificate you confirmed (or a code you
        scanned from your own computer), and signing out
        stops it.
      </p>

      <h2 class="h3">Permissions</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Notifications</strong>, to show playback controls in the notification
          shade and on the lock screen.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Foreground service (media playback) and wake lock</strong>, so music keeps
          playing with the screen off.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Internet and network state</strong>, used only for the connections
          described under Network.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Music and audio</strong> (on Android 12 and older, read storage), only if
          you turn on Find All Music Automatically, to add the songs on your device to your
          library.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Noctis does not request location, contacts, camera, microphone, phone, photo or
        video permissions.
      </p>

      <h2 class="h3">Backups</h2>
      <p>
        If you have Android backup turned on, Android may include the app’s local data (your
        library index and artwork, including the song list and covers from your computer if
        you signed in, album descriptions, artist photos, artist logos, lyrics found online,
        favourites, playlists, play history, play queue, your scrobbling connections and
        settings) in your device backup to your Google account. Google handles that backup
        under your account; the developer cannot see it. Your account link and downloaded
        songs are never included. Turn off backup in Android’s settings to exclude the rest.
      </p>

      <h2 class="h3">Deleting your data</h2>
      <p>
        Everything Noctis stores is on your device. Uninstalling the app, or clearing its
        storage in Android’s settings, deletes all of it. Noctis keeps nothing on the
        services it asks for descriptions, photos, lyrics or logos; how they handle their
        own server logs is covered by their privacy policies. Plays sent to Last.fm or
        ListenBrainz stay in your account there until you delete them on that service. Your
        music files are never changed or deleted by uninstalling.
      </p>
      <p>
        Signing out (Settings → Account) removes your computer’s songs from the phone’s
        library and asks your computer to revoke this device’s key, and can also delete the
        downloaded songs. On your computer, Settings → Account &amp; Devices lists this
        phone and can remove it. What your phone sent to your computer (plays, favourites,
        playlist changes) lives in your computer’s own Noctis library, under your control.
      </p>

      <h2 class="h3">Children</h2>
      <p>
        Noctis is not directed at children under 13.
      </p>

      <h2 class="h3">Changes and contact</h2>
      <p>
        If this policy changes, this page is updated and its date changes. Questions: open
        an issue at <a href={links.issues}>{links.issues}</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Community slot="footer" />
</Base>

<style>
